01:07:09.560 --> 01:07:10.780
But if the argument is that

01:07:10.780 --> 01:07:12.820
the bubble thing being ridiculous is wrong,

01:07:12.900 --> 01:07:15.340
I disagree. I think that it's ridiculous, and that's great.

01:07:15.500 --> 01:07:17.680
I think it's hilariously ridiculous.

01:07:17.700 --> 01:07:21.060
I love that. Yeah, the bubble thing is fine with me

01:07:21.060 --> 01:07:22.580
because, like, that's water,

01:07:22.580 --> 01:07:23.700
and he's an alien,

01:07:23.700 --> 01:07:25.740
so he's not used to, you know,

01:07:25.820 --> 01:07:28.580
like, the bacteria in our water,

01:07:28.660 --> 01:07:29.740
and that killed him. Immediately.

01:07:30.100 --> 01:07:34.820
It killed him immediately. Yeah, exactly. Well, 14 shots of bubbles.

01:07:35.100 --> 01:07:37.060
But, like, bubble, water is life for us,

01:07:37.140 --> 01:07:38.640
but he is, like, this, like, weird alien.

01:07:38.780 --> 01:07:39.660
Water is death for him.

01:07:39.760 --> 01:07:42.260
I don't know. You should have. I mean, my opinion,

01:07:42.540 --> 01:07:44.100
you should have only been able to beat him

01:07:44.100 --> 01:07:45.020
with Omni Slash.

01:07:46.080 --> 01:07:47.200
Yeah. Is that a flare?

01:07:47.320 --> 01:07:48.300
Is that a flare on me?

01:07:48.740 --> 01:07:51.380
Or, if they really wanted to insult the player,

01:07:51.640 --> 01:07:52.520
right, that, you know,

01:07:52.620 --> 01:07:54.380
Dr. Wily would strip you

01:07:54.380 --> 01:07:56.120
of all of your powers

01:07:56.120 --> 01:07:58.620
and be like, oh, you thought those power-ups

01:07:58.620 --> 01:07:59.480
made you stronger?

01:07:59.760 --> 01:08:01.940
Like, I control those power-ups,

01:08:01.980 --> 01:08:04.140
and I'm deactivating them or something, right?

01:08:04.160 --> 01:08:05.640
He created those robot masters.

01:08:05.780 --> 01:08:07.040
At least that would feel like

01:08:07.040 --> 01:08:08.860
a bit more of a fully formed idea.

01:08:09.520 --> 01:08:10.380
Yes. It wouldn't be fun.

01:08:10.380 --> 01:08:11.380
This just feels ridiculous.

01:08:12.880 --> 01:08:14.740
Yeah. Yeah, I like the idea of, yeah,

01:08:15.020 --> 01:08:16.120
somehow the final battle

01:08:16.120 --> 01:08:18.420
should incorporate everything you've learned.

01:08:19.420 --> 01:08:20.560
I totally agree with that.

01:08:20.580 --> 01:08:21.480
Or not. Just, yeah,

01:08:21.580 --> 01:08:23.320
just make it this way you only have your blaster,

01:08:23.320 --> 01:08:25.540
or you only have the platforming power-ups.

01:08:25.580 --> 01:08:26.660
You have to keep dodging

01:08:26.660 --> 01:08:28.960
all of his shit until he depletes

01:08:28.960 --> 01:08:29.820
all of his energy

01:08:29.820 --> 01:08:31.280
by shooting so much

01:08:31.280 --> 01:08:33.320
of his life projectiles at you.

01:08:33.380 --> 01:08:34.700
Yeah, honestly, I think that

01:08:34.700 --> 01:08:36.880
the best solution is,

01:08:37.020 --> 01:08:38.280
to me, I think that

01:08:38.280 --> 01:08:39.400
it's actually not a bad idea

01:08:39.400 --> 01:08:41.340
that, like, when he changes color,

01:08:41.360 --> 01:08:42.740
you have to figure out

01:08:42.740 --> 01:08:43.940
what to use against that color

01:08:43.940 --> 01:08:45.980
or something. Like, that's not a bad idea,

01:08:46.780 --> 01:08:48.580
in my opinion. I think that that would be better

01:08:48.580 --> 01:08:50.240
than the way they did it. But, you know,

01:08:50.300 --> 01:08:51.240
just to continue playing

01:08:51.240 --> 01:08:53.180
the devil's advocate, you could look at it

01:08:53.180 --> 01:08:54.620
as, like, the whole final fortress

01:08:54.620 --> 01:08:57.380
was to, like, learn to use all your power-ups.

01:08:57.980 --> 01:08:59.420
And he's just the bubbles.

01:08:59.640 --> 01:09:00.560
And he's just, yeah,

01:09:00.560 --> 01:09:01.760
the bubble one, and he just happens

01:09:01.760 --> 01:09:05.040
to be the last one. But, like, yeah,

01:09:05.120 --> 01:09:05.960
I agree that

01:09:05.960 --> 01:09:07.320
the final battle

01:09:07.320 --> 01:09:09.360
should be like, okay, now put everything to use.

01:09:09.620 --> 01:09:10.780
I really think they had,

01:09:10.860 --> 01:09:12.840
like, a really, you know,

01:09:12.900 --> 01:09:14.400
and I'm speaking with hyperbole here,

01:09:14.420 --> 01:09:15.920
but, like, I feel like they had

01:09:15.920 --> 01:09:18.320
an amazing, like,

01:09:18.600 --> 01:09:20.440
game here, and then they just

01:09:20.440 --> 01:09:21.960
threw out the playbook

01:09:21.960 --> 01:09:23.440
on the final boss.

01:09:23.580 --> 01:09:25.160
They said, like, they almost, like,

01:09:25.200 --> 01:09:26.700
took a break for six months

01:09:27.060 --> 01:09:28.060
and then said, like, oh, yeah,

01:09:28.060 --> 01:09:28.960
we never finished programming

01:09:28.960 --> 01:09:30.140
that final boss. I don't know,

01:09:30.200 --> 01:09:31.460
hit him with some bubbles. Like,

01:09:31.560 --> 01:09:33.180
it's just so far away

01:09:33.180 --> 01:09:34.100
from the ideas

01:09:34.740 --> 01:09:36.620
of what Mega Man is

01:09:36.620 --> 01:09:38.280
and, like, what the game was about

01:09:38.280 --> 01:09:40.280
that I'm just confused by it.

01:09:40.340 --> 01:09:42.560
And obviously, like, that's a little extreme,

01:09:43.660 --> 01:09:45.060
but I have to say it.

01:09:45.140 --> 01:09:46.100
Like, I really think that

01:09:46.100 --> 01:09:47.860
the Dr. Wily final boss thing

01:09:47.860 --> 01:09:48.800
is just a mistake.

01:09:49.000 --> 01:09:50.260
The other five levels

01:09:50.260 --> 01:09:51.640
leading up to it are so much better.

01:09:51.840 --> 01:09:53.400
One of the biggest mistakes

01:09:53.400 --> 01:09:54.580
in game design.

01:09:55.140 --> 01:09:57.000
I mean, once you guys discovered

01:09:57.000 --> 01:09:58.060
the bubbles,

01:09:58.120 --> 01:09:59.000
did you ever switch

01:09:59.000 --> 01:10:00.820
to another power up?

01:10:02.180 --> 01:10:03.840
Never. No, I'm serious.

01:10:03.940 --> 01:10:04.960
Like, in that battle,

01:10:05.100 --> 01:10:06.220
once you discovered that the bubbles

01:10:06.220 --> 01:10:07.800
are what hurts him, did you ever then go back?

01:10:07.940 --> 01:10:09.560
I didn't actually get to this point.

01:10:09.920 --> 01:10:10.900
What you realize

01:10:10.900 --> 01:10:12.620
once you've taken some damage down

01:10:12.620 --> 01:10:13.700
is that not only

01:10:13.700 --> 01:10:14.640
are all your other weapons

01:10:14.640 --> 01:10:16.160
useless, they all heal him

01:10:16.160 --> 01:10:17.040
back to full health.

01:10:17.240 --> 01:10:18.640
If you hit him with anything else,

01:10:18.760 --> 01:10:19.780
even your regular blaster,

01:10:19.980 --> 01:10:20.860
he's back to full health

01:10:20.860 --> 01:10:22.120
immediately. One hit.

01:10:22.820 --> 01:10:23.920
Why wouldn't he just

01:10:23.920 --> 01:10:25.620
design the bubble to do that as well?

01:10:26.080 --> 01:10:27.380
Maybe it was an oversight

01:10:27.380 --> 01:10:28.580
of his part. His hubris

01:10:28.580 --> 01:10:29.380
is what killed him.

01:10:29.380 --> 01:10:30.560
He didn't think water

01:10:30.560 --> 01:10:31.620
could possibly hurt him.

01:10:31.780 --> 01:10:32.700
Also, here's the thing.

01:10:32.860 --> 01:10:34.800
If we're out in the vacuum of space,

01:10:34.800 --> 01:10:36.160
you can't have bubbles.

01:10:36.540 --> 01:10:38.160
It would just, like,

01:10:38.400 --> 01:10:39.740
non-bubble, you know?

01:10:39.780 --> 01:10:40.940
That's a great point, Sean.

01:10:41.100 --> 01:10:41.920
Maybe it's, you know,

01:10:41.920 --> 01:10:42.900
there's nothing inside it.

01:10:42.940 --> 01:10:44.040
It's just a circle of water.

01:10:44.680 --> 01:10:46.500
It doesn't have to be filled with air, right?

01:10:46.560 --> 01:10:47.480
Well, what's expanding

01:10:47.480 --> 01:10:48.920
the bubble to be a bubble?

01:10:49.060 --> 01:10:50.460
Well, or the air that you,

01:10:50.640 --> 01:10:52.280
I mean, you generate this.

01:10:52.300 --> 01:10:54.140
Well, now there's too much

01:10:54.140 --> 01:10:55.600
air in the bubble and it's going to pop

01:10:55.600 --> 01:10:56.620
because of the vacuum of space.

01:10:56.720 --> 01:10:58.140
Yeah, it pops right on him. Think before you talk.

01:10:58.820 --> 01:10:59.980
It pops right on him.

01:11:00.060 --> 01:11:01.500
You can have some sort of bubble.

01:11:01.580 --> 01:11:03.200
Maybe there's a field around it,

01:11:03.260 --> 01:11:05.140
you know? It's a force field bubble.

01:11:05.260 --> 01:11:06.080
Is that what it is, Joe?

01:11:06.140 --> 01:11:07.240
A force field bubble, yeah. Yeah, lead.

01:11:07.440 --> 01:11:08.940
They're made out of lead. They're not going to pop.

01:11:11.020 --> 01:11:13.480
Come on. You got me there.

01:11:13.500 --> 01:11:15.460
Tell me it is my brain. Yeah, that's right.

01:11:15.720 --> 01:11:51.660
Yeah. One thing we missed, we didn't talk about the energy tanks at all.

01:11:51.800 --> 01:11:55.940
And those are optional things that you can find across the various stages.

01:11:56.100 --> 01:11:58.800
They usually make it a little bit of a platforming challenge to find them.

01:11:59.060 --> 01:12:05.740
And you can bank these energy tanks, up to four of them, and they will completely replenish your health.

01:12:05.800 --> 01:12:11.740
And if you use them any time outside of a boss fight, you have to really question what you're doing.

01:12:11.740 --> 01:12:16.320
You have to really sit down with yourself and ask yourself, why am I replenishing my health now?

01:12:17.100 --> 01:12:22.980
But if you're not, if you're just using it during a boss fight, wow, what a great addition to have these energy tanks.

01:12:23.440 --> 01:12:33.320
Kind of makes me wish that they also had power-up tanks that you could store in case you were going up against the crash bomber turrets or Dr.

01:12:33.520 --> 01:12:34.740
Wily's alien forms.

01:12:34.740 --> 01:12:39.220
This way it was like, oh, if I'm out of bubbles, at least I saved a power-up tank.

01:12:39.300 --> 01:12:40.440
Yeah. Right.

01:12:40.780 --> 01:12:46.860
This box, yeah, this convenient box of bubbles from Toys R Us, because that's where people buy their bubbles.

01:12:47.700 --> 01:12:48.720
At least I have that.

01:12:49.500 --> 01:12:54.160
Well, see, I thought when I first saw those, because I actually didn't notice I was collecting those.

01:12:54.300 --> 01:12:56.260
Like, I didn't notice I was gathering those until towards the end.

01:12:56.440 --> 01:13:03.600
So I had a couple at the end, and I thought that they were going to let me, yeah, like you said, either give all my health back,

01:13:03.740 --> 01:13:07.180
or I thought I could, like, select it and then select whichever power-up I want.

01:13:07.220 --> 01:13:09.220
I thought I could just fill up one bar of anything.

01:13:09.220 --> 01:13:12.500
I did sort of think that's how it worked the first time I used it as well.

01:13:13.400 --> 01:13:17.660
But, I mean, that was a godsend for me.

01:13:17.880 --> 01:13:22.980
I wouldn't have been able to get as far in this game if I didn't have those energy tanks.

01:13:22.980 --> 01:13:28.860
And I think it also speaks to, like, how the difference between Mega Man 1 and 2.

01:13:29.380 --> 01:13:37.240
Like, Mega Man 1 had a lot of weird, like, holdovers from an arcade experience that never existed.

01:13:37.960 --> 01:13:39.980
Like, you know, the points.

01:13:41.380 --> 01:13:43.880
And there's nothing, the points are just there.

01:13:45.900 --> 01:13:51.320
And they drop that, and now they add something that is really only useful for consoles.

01:13:51.780 --> 01:13:54.040
That and the password system.

01:13:54.140 --> 01:13:57.540
It seems like, yeah, between 1 and 2, they finally figured out how to be a console game.

01:13:57.540 --> 01:13:59.940
But, like, having these energy things.

01:14:00.200 --> 01:14:05.380
Like, you would never see an arcade game where you could just use an item to get back to full health.

01:14:06.960 --> 01:14:09.040
Right, yeah. You're losing money.

01:14:09.360 --> 01:14:13.620
Yeah. So, I thought that was just a cool little aside there.

01:14:13.620 --> 01:14:16.500
And what about that creative password system?

01:14:16.880 --> 01:14:22.660
No more remembering random strings of letters or numbers or a combination of letters and numbers.

01:14:22.920 --> 01:14:25.300
Instead, just fill out this .matrix.

01:14:25.900 --> 01:14:27.580
Remember this QR code.

01:14:27.920 --> 01:14:33.400
Yeah, I like it better now in the age where I have a camera in my pocket all the time and I can just snap a photo of it.

01:14:33.880 --> 01:14:40.700
It'd probably be easier to remember, like, a numerical or letter code if I was just committing it to memory, though.

01:14:41.480 --> 01:14:44.360
So, not that I feel like anyone just committed it to memory.

01:14:45.020 --> 01:14:49.460
Yeah. Now, Sean, you're, like, the mathematician of the group.

01:14:49.920 --> 01:14:53.300
Mathmagician. Mathmagician of the group because you make magic with math.

01:14:53.440 --> 01:14:55.800
You killed it on Sesame Street 1, 2, 3.

01:14:56.060 --> 01:14:58.800
Yeah. That's true, except for your opinion on the menu.

01:14:59.000 --> 01:15:00.700
But, here's the thing.

01:15:01.300 --> 01:15:09.000
Am I wrong in thinking that, like, this is a pretty easy password table to brute force into, like, getting some kind of result?

01:15:09.000 --> 01:15:11.380
I, you know, you're right.

01:15:11.640 --> 01:15:15.220
Like, there are only two options for each field.

01:15:16.800 --> 01:15:18.200
Right? It's, like, on or off.

01:15:18.440 --> 01:15:19.340
Yeah, on or off. Like, dead or alive.

01:15:19.680 --> 01:15:27.140
Instead of 24 or however many, I guess, 30, I don't know how many letters are in the alphabet.

01:15:27.140 --> 01:15:29.000
You don't know me. But, um...

01:15:29.720 --> 01:15:31.160
26. 26 letters in the alphabet.

01:15:31.160 --> 01:15:34.520
Plus 10. So, 36 characters for a password system.

01:15:34.680 --> 01:15:39.300
Like, yeah, you could definitely brute force this to, I guess, end up at any random point in the game.

01:15:39.320 --> 01:15:40.840
But why would you do that?

01:15:41.960 --> 01:15:45.180
Right, right. I guess, you know, it depends on what age you are.

01:15:45.400 --> 01:15:47.440
You know, if you're 6, that could be amazing.

01:15:48.540 --> 01:15:52.000
Yeah. If you're 36, that could still be amazing.

01:15:52.160 --> 01:15:53.780
It just depends on how you want to play the game.

01:15:54.000 --> 01:15:55.540
Maybe that's, like, a fun challenge.

01:15:55.720 --> 01:16:00.340
Is, like, enter a password and just see where you wind up and you have to do the rest of the game from there.

01:16:00.840 --> 01:16:03.060
That's fun. Nah, I don't know about that.

01:16:03.140 --> 01:16:04.840
But that could be, like, a fun speedrunning challenge.

01:16:06.320 --> 01:16:12.020
Like, somebody starts a little bit ahead or different enemies or bosses are already defeated.

01:16:12.020 --> 01:16:14.080
So, like, then they have to race each other to the finish.

01:16:14.220 --> 01:16:17.980
Yeah, but that can lead to being, like, really unfair really easily.

01:16:18.120 --> 01:16:19.280
Oh, absolutely. Yeah.

01:16:19.820 --> 01:16:20.680
Forget I ever said it.

01:16:20.740 --> 01:16:22.120
I will. Already done.

01:16:22.200 --> 01:16:29.900
Cool. One note on development because I don't want to get into, you know, like, the whole, like, making of the games and everything like that.

01:16:29.900 --> 01:16:33.380
Because, obviously, like, a game like Mega Man 2 has a lot of information about it.

01:16:33.620 --> 01:16:38.080
But this is the game where Inafune joined Mega Man 2.

01:16:38.100 --> 01:16:41.740
And he is now the guy who made that, like, Mighty No.

01:16:41.840 --> 01:16:46.400
9 game that everybody hated but backed with millions of dollars.

01:16:47.140 --> 01:16:49.440
He is, like, known as the creator of Mega Man.

01:16:49.540 --> 01:16:50.720
He is not the creator of Mega Man.

01:16:50.760 --> 01:16:54.700
He's just the guy who made Mega Man what we think of Mega Man today.

01:16:54.700 --> 01:17:03.820
So, I guess Mega Man 2 is a game a lot of people still really love because they would fund any project that this guy makes to the point where they let him make Mighty No.

01:17:03.900 --> 01:17:12.080
9. And the developers allowed input from the general public in Japan for boss designs throughout the game.

01:17:12.380 --> 01:17:18.360
Capcom received 8,370 boss submissions for Mega Man 2.

01:17:19.040 --> 01:17:22.540
And they kind of gave an answer that sounded like they went with none of them.

01:17:22.760 --> 01:17:24.060
But that would be, like, impossible.

01:17:24.420 --> 01:17:27.700
Oh, really? Somebody else comes up with Wood Man.

01:17:27.840 --> 01:17:30.000
I thought that that was really cool.

01:17:30.060 --> 01:17:42.120
And I just recently heard, I don't know if it was true or not, but I heard that ever since Mega Man 2, like, everything but Mega Man 1, all of the bosses were fan designed.

01:17:42.860 --> 01:17:43.700
Is that not true?

01:17:44.720 --> 01:17:50.380
Well, I think what they're trying to say is, like, you know, that they don't say, like, you know, you're the winner or something like that.

01:17:50.540 --> 01:17:55.380
Instead, they, like, they look at the fan designs, draw inspiration from them, and then make their own.

01:17:55.560 --> 01:17:57.820
So, basically, that's their way of saying that they're stealing from them.

01:17:58.880 --> 01:18:01.500
Yeah, I assume that they, like, designed on top of it.

01:18:01.560 --> 01:18:05.080
But I thought that they were, like, hey, send us your idea and someone sends in Wood Man.

01:18:05.280 --> 01:18:06.120
And they're, like, oh, great.

01:18:06.240 --> 01:18:07.700
I'll make these couple changes and put it in.

01:18:07.780 --> 01:18:12.320
But, like, I thought it was, like, largely still that person's design.

01:18:12.440 --> 01:18:13.320
I could be totally wrong.

01:18:14.680 --> 01:18:15.580
Got it. Okay.

01:18:15.720 --> 01:18:16.680
I don't have enough on that.

01:18:16.820 --> 01:18:22.640
So, maybe the friendship got a little bigger over time, you know, like the trust with the community.

01:18:23.280 --> 01:18:28.320
But in this game, they say that they took inspiration and made the designs their own.

01:18:28.760 --> 01:18:29.920
Gotcha. That makes sense.

01:18:30.600 --> 01:18:31.740
Sequels and spinoffs.

01:18:32.240 --> 01:18:35.580
I don't know what you want to hear from this because there's a lot of Mega Man games.

01:18:35.720 --> 01:18:38.020
And I think we've gone over them all and we're going to play a bunch more.

01:18:38.160 --> 01:18:45.100
Don't forget we have Mega Man 3, Mega Man 4, Mega Man 5, and Mega Man 6 still left to play on this very console.

01:18:45.540 --> 01:18:51.580
Isn't that crazy? We're not going to see another Metroid game on this console, but we're going to get four more Mega Man games.

01:18:52.380 --> 01:18:53.220
I love it.

01:18:54.140 --> 01:18:56.080
Yeah, I guess that's good.

01:18:57.040 --> 01:18:58.920
Well, yeah, at least half of that is good.

01:18:59.320 --> 01:19:00.460
The Mega Man part.

01:19:00.960 --> 01:19:03.440
I'd be happy to see more Metroid games too, I guess.

01:19:03.680 --> 01:19:09.000
Do you guys, like, look forward to Mega Man 3, let alone Mega Man 6?

01:19:09.000 --> 01:19:14.240
Like, would you say that you're more excited for Mega Man 3 or Mega Man 6, if that makes sense?

01:19:14.240 --> 01:19:25.360
I mean, just knowing how little the core design philosophy of these games has changed, like, I can't say that, like, I can't wait to see what they improve on.

01:19:25.520 --> 01:19:34.040
But I guess I'm interested to see, like, oh, what kind of goofy weapons slash bosses are they going to have now?

01:19:34.300 --> 01:19:38.280
So that's kind of how I'm feeling.

01:19:38.280 --> 01:19:40.720
Yeah, I mean, I'm excited for the next thing.

01:19:40.820 --> 01:19:52.060
And I do feel like that there is, like, a time and place, in my opinion, for, like, just making another iteration of your last game.

01:19:52.200 --> 01:20:00.840
Like, I think about this a lot with, like, I know people really want to, like, change, you know, some people really want, like, Pokemon to be, like, changed to, like, a new format.

01:20:01.000 --> 01:20:03.960
Add a defense button, like a defend button and things like that.

01:20:04.080 --> 01:20:06.340
Things that'll, or, like, make it, like, an action game.

01:20:06.680 --> 01:20:08.080
And, like, I think things like that are cool.

01:20:08.080 --> 01:20:09.760
If you make a side game that's like that.

01:20:09.760 --> 01:20:18.860
But I think that there is some merit into, like, hey, let's keep all of the core gameplay elements, keep it the same game, and just make a better one.

01:20:18.960 --> 01:20:20.860
Or make one with different, unique things.

01:20:20.940 --> 01:20:21.820
So people can always come back.

01:20:21.820 --> 01:20:23.980
When you play a Mega Man game, you know what you're getting into.

01:20:23.980 --> 01:20:27.340
But you're still discovering all these new things.

01:20:27.480 --> 01:20:31.300
It's like, hey, it's just like the last Mega Man, but now I have this power up, so it lets me do this.

01:20:31.620 --> 01:20:39.520
Whereas I feel like a lot of other game, like, franchises sometimes try to evolve too quickly into a whole new direction.

01:20:40.740 --> 01:20:44.040
And then that, like, makes it feel like it's a totally, you know, different game.

01:20:44.160 --> 01:20:45.580
I mean, Final Fantasy is another example.

01:20:45.800 --> 01:20:51.140
I mean, modern Final Fantasy, Final Fantasy XV, I'm sure it's a great game, and I'll play it at some point.

01:20:51.140 --> 01:20:55.160
But, like, when I sit down and play it, like, that does not scratch the itch of a Final Fantasy game.

01:20:55.300 --> 01:21:00.400
So, like, I respect that Mega Man will always be like, hey, more Mega Man with new ideas in it.

01:21:00.920 --> 01:21:02.780
Yeah, I kind of agree, yeah.

01:21:03.100 --> 01:21:07.120
And I'm sure there are, like, seasoned Mega Man fans listening to the episode right now.

01:21:07.140 --> 01:21:08.820
They're like, oh, Joe, if you only knew.

01:21:09.020 --> 01:21:10.900
Yeah, I can't wait to be disappointed.

01:21:10.900 --> 01:21:17.320
I don't know where it goes either, but I'm sure there's got to be a point where they lost, you know, the trust of the fans.

01:21:17.720 --> 01:21:18.840
I think you know.

01:21:20.100 --> 01:21:21.900
No, no, no. Well, you know, you have to think about it, right?

01:21:22.020 --> 01:21:25.500
Like, what Mega Man 8 was, like, PS1 times.

01:21:25.760 --> 01:21:30.720
Yeah. And then, like, Mega Man 9 took, like, all the way to the Wii to, like, get released.

01:21:30.840 --> 01:21:33.240
So, clearly, like, something happened there, right?

01:21:33.340 --> 01:21:39.900
Like, there was a rift, either whether it be sales or just design philosophy that, like, took them so long to come up with 9.

01:21:40.240 --> 01:21:43.880
But then there's also a huge gap between 9 and 10 and then 11.

01:21:43.880 --> 01:21:45.820
But they had all the X's, too, right?

01:21:46.060 --> 01:21:47.440
Like, those were. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:21:47.440 --> 01:21:48.820
But the X's also stop at 8.

01:21:48.920 --> 01:21:49.720
That's kind of interesting.

01:21:50.000 --> 01:21:51.200
That sounds like a conspiracy.

01:21:51.580 --> 01:21:53.080
Like, what's wrong with the number 8 in Mega Man?

01:21:53.680 --> 01:21:56.540
And that's why Inafune went on to make Mighty No.

01:21:56.680 --> 01:21:58.200
9. To skip 8 entirely.

01:21:58.200 --> 01:22:02.920
Yeah, see, yeah, just skip the number 8 and go right to 9.

01:22:03.900 --> 01:22:10.420
All right, and then, you know, in terms of Mega Man 2 stuff, there is a Mega Man 2 on the Game Boy, but it is not Mega Man 2.

01:22:10.600 --> 01:22:11.700
It is not this game.

01:22:11.700 --> 01:22:12.780
It is not these bosses.

01:22:12.780 --> 01:22:15.720
It involves Dr.

01:22:15.860 --> 01:22:31.280
Wiley coming back again in an attempt to take over the world, and this time he's stolen the experimental time skimmer from the world's Kronos Institute and uses it to travel 37.426, but that can't be right, right?

01:22:31.380 --> 01:22:35.760
Like, it's probably 37,426 years into the future.

01:22:35.880 --> 01:22:38.760
But I guess you could go 37.426 years.

01:22:38.760 --> 01:22:43.940
That might just be a European thing, because they use dots instead of commas.

01:22:44.140 --> 01:22:45.140
Got it. All right.

01:22:46.100 --> 01:22:53.060
And, yeah, you know, Mega Man has got to go into the future as well and take down Dr.

01:22:53.160 --> 01:22:54.660
Wiley. A lot going on with this Wiley guy.

01:22:54.680 --> 01:22:56.420
He's an alien. He's a time traveler.

01:22:56.660 --> 01:23:01.260
Dr. Wiley. He sounds a lot like, yeah, he's like Biff from Back to the Future.

01:23:01.920 --> 01:23:03.280
Yeah. Wait, Biff was an alien?

01:23:04.180 --> 01:23:06.920
No, Biff was an alien in Back to the Future 4.

01:23:06.920 --> 01:23:08.140
He just didn't read the script.

01:23:08.140 --> 01:23:09.000
Oh, okay.

01:23:09.660 --> 01:23:10.660
No, no, no, it's not true.

01:23:10.720 --> 01:23:11.640
I didn't believe you.

01:23:11.820 --> 01:23:13.800
There is no script for Back to the Future 4.

01:23:13.900 --> 01:23:16.500
Don't look it up. What else?

01:23:16.640 --> 01:23:25.080
Oh, Mega Man 2 also got, in 2019 for its 30th anniversary, it got a re-release of the NES cartridge from Capcom.

01:23:25.260 --> 01:23:26.080
That's pretty cool.

01:23:27.220 --> 01:23:28.640
Yeah. I like the actual...

01:23:28.640 --> 01:23:32.600
Like an officially licensed NES cart of Mega Man 2.

01:23:33.220 --> 01:23:34.180
What year was this?

01:23:35.240 --> 01:23:36.280
2019. Wow.

01:23:36.440 --> 01:23:39.100
Oh, my God. Isn't that crazy?

01:23:39.320 --> 01:23:42.140
Yeah. I mean, what's the point of that?

01:23:43.360 --> 01:23:44.700
It's cool, I guess.

01:23:46.280 --> 01:23:48.180
Why would I ever want to do that, Mike?

01:23:49.140 --> 01:23:50.440
Yeah. I don't know.

01:23:50.500 --> 01:23:53.080
I guess they made it cost a lot of money.

01:23:53.220 --> 01:23:58.200
So people thought, I'll buy this now while it's a collector's item and it will skyrocket in value.

01:23:58.500 --> 01:24:01.720
And then it turns out they made just enough for all the Mega Man 2 fans to have it.

01:24:01.880 --> 01:24:04.460
Yeah. So nobody else is seeking it.

01:24:04.720 --> 01:24:05.860
But I think it's blue.

01:24:06.120 --> 01:24:07.240
I think the cartridge is blue.

01:24:07.420 --> 01:24:08.820
So does that intrigue you?

01:24:08.860 --> 01:24:09.720
I like blue stuff.

01:24:09.800 --> 01:24:15.280
It changes things. I mean, I guess it also helps preserve it a little bit in its hopefully original form.

01:24:15.280 --> 01:24:16.520
I'm assuming they didn't change anything.

01:24:16.680 --> 01:24:17.820
So, I mean, that's nice. Yeah.

01:24:17.820 --> 01:24:20.600
But don't you think they just put like a retro pie in there?

01:24:20.640 --> 01:24:23.080
Yeah, probably. In the cartridge?

01:24:23.420 --> 01:24:25.460
You don't even have to. You don't even have to put it in your NES.

01:24:25.460 --> 01:24:28.600
You just play it on the cartridge itself.

01:24:28.760 --> 01:24:34.220
There's a screen on it. On the back of the cartridge is an LCD screen and you just play right off of that.

01:24:36.080 --> 01:24:37.380
All right. That's a bit of a tangent.

01:24:37.460 --> 01:24:39.760
But that's all I have for sequels and spin-offs really for Mega Man 2.

01:24:39.900 --> 01:24:43.680
I mean, this game has lots of re-releases but not that much changes.

01:24:43.680 --> 01:24:45.280
I'm sure there are subtle changes.

01:24:45.280 --> 01:24:49.200
I'm not going to get into it because we have gone too much into Mega Man 2.

01:24:49.320 --> 01:24:54.940
We've been talking for so long and we need to wrap this thing up on a thing we call the Essential Games List.

01:25:01.380 --> 01:25:02.780
Sean, you first.

01:25:03.000 --> 01:25:07.160
Give me your vote for Mega Man 2 on the Essential Games List.

01:25:08.140 --> 01:25:09.520
Mega Man 2.

01:25:10.040 --> 01:25:12.460
Huh? Like, look at that.

01:25:12.460 --> 01:25:15.260
Like, this is Mega Man 2.

01:25:17.460 --> 01:25:18.560
I don't know what I'm doing.

01:25:19.260 --> 01:25:23.480
I mean, we didn't even, like, talk about a lot of the other stuff with this game.

01:25:23.480 --> 01:25:28.440
Like, how iconic the music is and that's pretty much it.

01:25:29.820 --> 01:25:38.360
But I think that this is a game that sort of does live up to its reputation in terms of, like, why it has a legacy.

01:25:38.360 --> 01:25:42.820
I had a lot of, like, nitpicks with it.

01:25:42.880 --> 01:25:49.320
But I do think that quality-wise it is an improvement on an Essential Game.

01:25:50.060 --> 01:25:54.260
And it's not just, like, more of that Essential Game.

01:25:55.400 --> 01:25:59.300
So, I would say that this is also an Essential Game.

01:25:59.520 --> 01:26:00.780
Thank you for your vote, Sean.

01:26:01.060 --> 01:26:02.340
Joe, your vote.

01:26:02.700 --> 01:26:04.200
Mega Man 2.

01:26:04.580 --> 01:26:23.420
Huh. I think that a lot of the things that we complain about in other games when it comes to enemy placement and, like, level design and, you know, just problems that other games have with pacing and just, like, feeling like a balanced game.

01:26:24.040 --> 01:26:26.780
Mega Man 2 is, like, the antithesis of all that.

01:26:26.940 --> 01:26:29.880
Like, it is, I think that this is, like, a good example.

01:26:30.100 --> 01:26:39.920
Even today, you know, in 2021, to, like, point to as, like, as a template or, like, a good example of how to do good gameplay.

01:26:40.220 --> 01:26:43.360
And I think that that, like, that shows throughout the whole game.

01:26:43.480 --> 01:26:47.260
I mean, every level, you're, you know, you have to think about what to do next.

01:26:47.260 --> 01:26:50.760
But it never feels, like, impossible or rarely feels impossible.

01:26:52.700 --> 01:26:55.240
And the enemies are all, like, thought out where they are.

01:26:55.320 --> 01:26:59.020
You have to really think about, like, how you navigate through this world.

01:26:59.260 --> 01:27:10.020
And I think that that adds so much to the fun, you know, than just, like, the power-ups and the enemy design and everything, which are all also fantastic.

01:27:11.560 --> 01:27:15.620
And I also think, yes, we do have a Mega Man game on the Essential Games list.

01:27:15.700 --> 01:27:20.300
I know sometimes we kind of go in this direction of, like, well, do we need two of them on there?

01:27:20.300 --> 01:27:25.040
But I think that that is when one, like, makes the other obsolete.

01:27:25.480 --> 01:27:33.480
And in this situation, I feel like it is sort of what I was alluding to earlier, where it's, like, it's an improvement on that original game.

01:27:34.020 --> 01:27:38.880
It doesn't, you know, both are using this formula that works.

01:27:38.900 --> 01:27:42.100
And I think that it is better than the original game, but it doesn't take away from that.

01:27:42.140 --> 01:27:43.220
It doesn't replace it.

01:27:45.100 --> 01:27:56.060
So I guess I'm also, like, explaining why I don't want to eventually take Mega Man 1 off the Essential Games list, while also saying that I want to put Mega Man 2 on the Essential Games list in a roundabout way.

01:27:56.140 --> 01:27:57.600
So, yes, I'm voting it Essential.

01:27:57.900 --> 01:28:00.480
Wow. And that means it's an Essential game.

01:28:01.100 --> 01:28:02.900
I still get to give my vote, of course.

01:28:03.020 --> 01:28:03.880
But you don't need to.

01:28:03.940 --> 01:28:06.260
It's in. Yeah. It's in.

01:28:06.260 --> 01:28:10.660
The only thing I guess I'll say is Mega Man 2.

01:28:11.120 --> 01:28:17.720
Huh. You know, here's the thing.

01:28:17.780 --> 01:28:18.820
Here's the thing about Mega Man 2.

01:28:19.060 --> 01:28:20.380
It's like a no-brainer, right?

01:28:20.480 --> 01:28:25.020
If Mega Man 1 is an Essential game and Mega Man 2 is better than Mega Man 1, well, then what do you have?

01:28:25.100 --> 01:28:26.180
Of course you have an Essential game.

01:28:27.760 --> 01:28:32.440
Never, never since, like, I first sat down and played the game.

01:28:32.440 --> 01:28:37.080
And I played this game, you know, before Nostalgia, so I guess that's a little cheating.

01:28:37.800 --> 01:28:39.240
But I knew this was a good game.

01:28:39.280 --> 01:28:41.640
I knew it was a great game. And I knew it was going to be on the Essential Games list.

01:28:41.800 --> 01:28:46.160
I'm really happy that after playing it the last couple weeks, that reconfirmed it for me.

01:28:46.800 --> 01:28:51.680
Sean, you were talking about how, like, you know, we just didn't talk about, like, a lot of the iconic stuff that this game revolves around.

01:28:51.780 --> 01:28:52.780
And I think that's true, right?

01:28:52.820 --> 01:28:54.360
Like, yes, the music is amazing.

01:28:54.660 --> 01:28:58.220
And even, like, the Mega Man in Super Smash Bros.

01:28:59.660 --> 01:29:01.360
is based off of the Mega Man 2.

01:29:01.360 --> 01:29:02.940
I mean, all the power-ups and stuff.

01:29:03.060 --> 01:29:04.660
That's where I saw all this stuff first.

01:29:05.400 --> 01:29:11.260
Right. It's like, you know, it's in the, like, this game is the DNA of a Mega Man game.

01:29:11.460 --> 01:29:19.440
And regardless of if, like, you know, we try to use the argument again for Mega Man 3, 4, 5, where it's like, yeah, it's Mega Man again, even better.

01:29:19.560 --> 01:29:27.040
It's like, something about Mega Man 2 is just, like, it's larger than just an improvement.

01:29:27.040 --> 01:29:30.580
It is now, for me, the Mega Man game.

01:29:30.860 --> 01:29:33.680
That this is the standard I'll be holding them up against, too.

01:29:33.960 --> 01:29:35.480
And I don't know if I agree with Joe.

01:29:35.680 --> 01:29:49.360
I think Mega Man 1, you know, on my outlook, on my horizon, the best of 1989, I'm looking at Mega Man 1, and I'm saying, you might be next, kid.

01:29:49.980 --> 01:29:51.300
Because here's the thing.

01:29:52.120 --> 01:29:59.340
Mega Man 1 had a lot of frustrations, but was overall a great game for 1987.

01:30:00.480 --> 01:30:01.920
Or 1988, I believe.

01:30:02.720 --> 01:30:03.600
No, 1987.

01:30:04.060 --> 01:30:05.540
I'm going to say 87. I don't remember.

01:30:05.720 --> 01:30:06.760
But that's what we'll go with.

01:30:07.600 --> 01:30:13.020
Mega Man 2 still has its frustrations, but I had to really reach out for them.

01:30:13.020 --> 01:30:17.260
We were talking about things like, you know, yeah, the final boss doesn't quite deliver, right?

01:30:17.440 --> 01:30:21.700
But, like, is anybody throwing Bioshock off the Essential Games list because the final boss sucks?

01:30:21.700 --> 01:30:22.700
Like, I don't think so.

01:30:22.980 --> 01:30:27.340
And we were talking about, you know, movement's not quite there yet.

01:30:27.340 --> 01:30:35.640
It's like, yeah, but, like, for where we are in game design, it's not really a noticeable difference unless you're playing in 2021.

01:30:36.360 --> 01:30:49.260
The point here is that, like, the influence that this game has on this genre, on Mega Man games, and just on, like, if you're a fan of indie platformers that are coming out today, right?

01:30:49.320 --> 01:30:51.780
There's a good chance that you're going to love Mega Man 2.

01:30:52.380 --> 01:30:54.500
Is it kind of hard?

01:30:54.660 --> 01:30:55.880
Sure. Is it NES hard?

01:30:56.000 --> 01:30:56.840
I don't really think so.

01:30:56.960 --> 01:31:05.240
I think this is the kind of game where, like, you just have to play through a level a few times, and you'll have it down pat, and they give you enough health, and there is that easy mode.

01:31:05.240 --> 01:31:15.380
Honestly, if I'm having a good time with this game, you can too, because I'm not that great of a video gamer, and I had a great time playing Mega Man 2, and I felt good as Mega Man.

01:31:15.440 --> 01:31:16.540
And that's the most important thing.

01:31:16.600 --> 01:31:18.040
You're the Mega Man.

01:31:18.240 --> 01:31:22.100
You want to feel mega, which is why when you fight Dr.

01:31:22.160 --> 01:31:25.280
Wily, all of your attacks should do damage.

01:31:25.400 --> 01:31:27.740
You should be thumping on his butt.

01:31:27.960 --> 01:31:30.360
Like, you should be all over him.

01:31:31.800 --> 01:31:33.900
It's just Sean's, like, quiet, like, whoa.

01:31:33.900 --> 01:31:35.460
Oh, that got me.

01:31:37.620 --> 01:31:44.400
Mega Man 2 is absolutely on the Central Games list, and it only took us, like, 90 minutes to get there, but here it is.

01:31:44.860 --> 01:31:54.700
And I can only imagine that next week when we play Monster Party, we're going to go into just the same amount of in-depth analysis and reach the same kind of conclusions.

01:31:54.700 --> 01:31:56.060
Right, guys? I can't wait.

01:31:56.060 --> 01:32:00.140
Absolutely. Everybody's heard of NES's Monster Party.

01:32:00.360 --> 01:32:02.000
I haven't, actually.

01:32:03.360 --> 01:32:04.300
Oh, interesting.

01:32:04.560 --> 01:32:07.880
By Human Entertainment, the totally not-robot.

01:32:08.020 --> 01:32:09.420
That sounds suspicious.

01:32:10.300 --> 01:32:12.440
I think the monsters made that game.

01:32:12.880 --> 01:32:16.780
Yeah. Monster Party is the game that we're playing next week.

01:32:16.840 --> 01:32:23.060
And the reason I bring that up so soon after a Mega Man segue is because you should always want to know what game we're playing next because we want you to play along with us.

01:32:23.060 --> 01:32:34.740
If you listened to 90 minutes of Mega Man 2 and you didn't play Mega Man 2 leading up to this week or following this episode, I'm not saying that you're listening to the show wrong.

01:32:34.740 --> 01:32:37.460
I'm saying you're doing yourself a disservice.

01:32:37.900 --> 01:32:43.040
Mega Man 2 is a game worth playing, and next week we're going to find out if Monster Party is a game worth playing.

01:32:43.260 --> 01:32:48.200
Joe, anything you could say to the listeners to make them play Mega Man 2 this minute?

01:32:48.820 --> 01:33:03.100
I think that knowing that Sean and Mike voted it on and they didn't even vote Zelda 2 on, that means that this game must be like, I mean, it must be doing like godlike feats because I don't know what could, I don't know.

01:33:03.160 --> 01:33:06.160
I just feel like I need to bring up Legend of Zelda 2 every episode.

01:33:06.420 --> 01:33:08.340
So, take a listen.

01:33:08.500 --> 01:33:14.600
Take a listen and then let us know if you agree that it should be on there and more importantly let Sean and Mike know that they're wrong about Zelda 2.

01:33:15.220 --> 01:33:18.660
Yeah, I mean, Mega Man 2 is at least as good as Operation Wolf.

01:33:18.900 --> 01:33:23.520
Oh, at least. At least as good, according to our Essential Games list.

01:33:23.580 --> 01:33:33.120
Of course, that's not really how the list works, but if you have feedback and you want to write to us and let us know that we made a mistake or we didn't make a mistake and that we're a great podcast,

01:33:33.360 --> 01:33:36.740
the best way to do that is at NostalgiaCast on Twitter.

01:33:37.000 --> 01:33:38.760
That's where we're posting stuff all week.

01:33:38.760 --> 01:33:45.280
You better believe we posted some great Mega Man 2 stuff all week long and you better believe that people commented on that stuff too.

01:33:45.480 --> 01:33:48.840
Don't go and count the comments because, you know, we record the episodes in advance.

01:33:48.960 --> 01:33:52.960
I don't know how many people commented, but I'm sure we got some comments on some thoughts.

01:33:53.160 --> 01:34:03.000
And Mega Man 2 is a game that we'll probably keep using as a reference point like we do with Mega Man 1 and Super Mario Brothers and Legend of Zelda 2, Metroid, you name it.

01:34:03.000 --> 01:34:08.720
That's the kind of game that this is and that's why it's just going to be like in our nostalgia dictionaries now.

01:34:09.020 --> 01:34:11.100
Sean, parting words for the audience.

01:34:11.380 --> 01:34:21.960
All right. Just because you had a bad day at the office, that doesn't give you the right to park in a handicapped spot.

01:34:22.180 --> 01:34:25.260
It's really not cool.

01:34:25.260 --> 01:34:32.920
Like, I think, like, anybody who needs it is going to tell you that, like, hey, I'd really appreciate it if you wouldn't park in a handicapped spot.

01:34:33.500 --> 01:34:35.300
So, you know, just do that.

01:34:35.600 --> 01:34:39.400
Just be the bigger man and park in a regular spot.

01:34:40.380 --> 01:34:42.960
Is this something you've encountered recently, Sean?

01:34:43.240 --> 01:34:44.720
No, it's just something I've been thinking about.

01:34:45.260 --> 01:34:46.460
Oh, okay. Okay.

01:35:11.360 --> 01:35:12.840
No, no, no, no.

01:35:13.800 --> 01:35:15.120
You're adding that.

01:35:15.560 --> 01:35:16.820
It's just that he's back.

01:35:17.260 --> 01:35:18.100
Yeah, he's back.

01:35:18.360 --> 01:35:19.800
But, like, he's back.

01:35:20.200 --> 01:35:22.320
This episode is posting on October.

01:35:23.640 --> 01:35:24.960
Also, what is he adding?

01:35:24.960 --> 01:35:26.840
I'm adding extra A's in the back?

01:35:28.120 --> 01:35:29.240
Okay. I think it's okay.

01:35:30.440 --> 01:35:32.680
You want me to say Mega Man 2, he's back?

01:35:33.260 --> 01:35:35.580
No. I want you to do what you did.

01:35:36.800 --> 01:35:40.400
I want you to say Mega Man 2, I'm Klu Klu for Gaga.

01:35:46.540 --> 01:35:47.460
I still don't know.

